.SS "schema-level method control"
.TP
.B defaults.method_prefer
Global, engine-wide preference order for install methods, set once under
\fB[defaults]\fR. The older global \fBdefaults.method_order\fR spelling is a compatibility alias; there is no per-tool \fBmethod_order\fR alias — use
\fBmethod_prefer\fR/\fBmethod_only\fR below to override the order for a
single tool.
.TP
.B method_prefer
Per-tool list of methods to try first, before the defaults.
Example: \fBmethod_prefer = ["cargo"]\fR tries cargo before native.
.TP
.B method_only
Filters the tool's candidate methods: only the listed methods are tried,
in this order, and the native method is NOT attempted unless explicitly
listed — there is no native fallback. \fBdefaults.method_prefer\fR is
ignored for this tool.
.PP
Scalar/bool method shorthand may infer a native fallback. Explicit method
subtables do not; declare a native method explicitly when full-table syntax
should retain native fallback.

.SS "kind field (adapter dispatch override)"
.PP
The \fBkind\fR field overrides the adapter dispatch key inside a method block.
Normally the section key (e.g. \fBhttp-musl\fR) IS the kind. When you have two
methods that use the same adapter but differ only in their install source
(e.g. glibc vs musl builds), set \fBkind = "http"\fR so the second variant
resolves to the same adapter.
.PP
Example:
.PP
.nf
[tools.restic]
  [tools.restic.http-musl]
  kind = "http"
  url = "https://example.com/musl"
  when = { libc = ["musl"] }
.fi
.PP
When \fBkind\fR is absent, the section key acts as the kind (backward
compatible). Non-string values are silently ignored.
.SS "when condition (platform gate by method)"
.PP
The \fBwhen\fR field inside a method declaration restricts which platforms
that method applies to. The condition is evaluated against host facts detected
natively by depengine at runtime. Semantics: AND across fields, OR within
each list. Empty fields are ignored. Absent \fBwhen\fR = always try.
.PP
Available fields:
.TP
.B distro_family
List of distro families (resolved clans). Example: \fB["arch"]\fR matches
Arch, EndeavourOS, Manjaro, CachyOS. Common values: arch, debian, fedora,
alpine, gentoo, void, suse, macos, freebsd, openbsd, netbsd, termux, android.
.TP
.B distro_id
List of exact distro IDs (case-insensitive). Example: \fB["ubuntu",
"debian"]\fR matches exactly those, not Mint or Pop!_OS.
.TP
.B arch
List of architectures (\fBuname -m\fR). Example: \fB["x86_64", "aarch64"]\fR.
.TP
.B os
List of operating systems (lowercase \fBuname -s\fR). Example:
\fB["linux"]\fR, \fB["darwin"]\fR, \fB["freebsd"]\fR.
.TP
.B target_family
OS family. \fB["unix"]\fR matches linux, darwin, the BSDs and termux;
\fB["windows"]\fR matches the windows clan (native, MINGW/CYGWIN/MSYS).
Use this instead of listing every unix \fBos\fR value.
.TP
.B kernel
List of kernel versions (\fBuname -r\fR). Exact match (case-insensitive).
Example: \fB["6.7.0-arch", "6.8.0"]\fR.
.TP
.B libc
List of C libraries. Uses \fBprefix\fR matching: \fB["glibc"]\fR matches
"glibc 2.35". Values: glibc, musl, uclibc, freebsd-libc.
.TP
.B init_system
List of init systems. Values: systemd, openrc, runit, sysvinit.
.TP
.B is_wsl
Three-state boolean: \fBtrue\fR (WSL only), \fBfalse\fR (non-WSL only),
omitted (ignored). Detected via /proc/version or WSL_DISTRO_NAME.
.TP
.B is_container
Three-state boolean: \fBtrue\fR (container only), \fBfalse\fR (non-container
only), omitted (ignored). Detected via /.dockerenv, cgroup, etc.
.TP
.B is_android
Three-state boolean: \fBtrue\fR (Android/Termux only), \fBfalse\fR (non-Android
only), omitted (ignored). Detected via Termux markers or Android-specific
hardware/OS properties. Use this instead of \fBos = ["android"]\fR, which
does not match — Android is exposed as a fact, not an \fBos\fR value.
.PP
Examples:
.EX
# AUR only on Arch, HTTP elsewhere
[tools.DepartureMono]
  [tools.DepartureMono.aur]
  pkg  = "otf-departure-mono-nerd"
  when = { distro_family = ["arch"] }

  [tools.DepartureMono.http]
  url = "https://github.com/ryanoasis/nerd-fonts/releases/download/{latest}/DepartureMono.zip"

# Different binary per libc (glibc vs musl/Alpine)
[tools.restic]
  [tools.restic.http]
  url = "https://github.com/restic/restic/releases/download/{latest}/restic_{latest}_linux_{arch}.bz2"
  when = { arch = ["x86_64","aarch64"], os = ["linux"], libc = ["glibc"] }

  [tools.restic.http-musl]
  kind = "http"
  url = "https://github.com/restic/restic/releases/download/{latest}/restic_{latest}_linux_{arch}_musl.bz2"
  when = { arch = ["x86_64","aarch64"], os = ["linux"], libc = ["musl"] }

# Skip native in containers
[tools.neovim]
  [tools.neovim.native]
  when = { is_container = false }

  [tools.neovim.http]
  url = "https://github.com/neovim/neovim/releases/download/stable/nvim-linux-{arch}.tar.gz"
  when = { is_container = true }
.EE
.PP
Tip: run \fBdepengine why <tool>\fR to see why a method was
skipped (\fBskip_when\fR) or would be attempted.

.SS "ecosystem buckets (python/node shorthand)"
.PP
Buckets expand to all methods in that ecosystem at once, avoiding repetition
across \fBpip\fR, \fBpipx\fR and \fBuv\fR (python) or \fBnpm\fR, \fBpnpm\fR
and \fBbun\fR (node).
.TP
.B python
Expands to \fBpip\fR, \fBpipx\fR and \fBuv\fR.
.TP
.B node
Expands to \fBnpm\fR, \fBpnpm\fR and \fBbun\fR.
.PP
Bucket values accept three shapes:
.br
\fBtrue\fR \— each method uses the tool name as \fBpkg\fR.
.br
\fB"string"\fR \— each method gets the string as \fBpkg\fR.
.br
\fB{ pkg = ..., when = ... }\fR \— each method gets a clone of the config map.
.PP
Examples:
.EX
ruff = { python = true }               # ≡ { pip = "ruff", pipx = "ruff", uv = "ruff" }
organize = { python = "organize-tool" } # ≡ pip/pipx/uv with pkg="organize-tool"
prettier = { node = true }             # ≡ { npm = "prettier", pnpm = "prettier", bun = "prettier" }
.EE
.PP
Explicit methods are NOT overridden by the bucket:
\fBorganize = { pip = "organize-tool", python = true }\fR keeps
\fBpip = "organize-tool"\fR and only expands \fBpipx\fR/\fBuv\fR.
Use \fBpython = false\fR to disable expansion.

.SH EXAMPLES
.SS "Build and validate"
.EX
go build -o depengine .
./depengine validate
.EE
.SS "Validate with environment check and JSON output"
.EX
./depengine validate --check-env --format=json
.EE
.SS "Install all tools from a custom schema"
.EX
./depengine install --schema my-tools.toml
.EE
.SS "Install a single tool"
.EX
./depengine install --only nvim
.EE
.SS "Dry run with sorted output"
.EX
./depengine install --diagnose
.EE
.SS "Install with profile (desktop-only)"
.EX
./depengine install --profile=desktop
.EE
.SS "Check if a tool is installed"
.EX
./depengine check nvim
.EE
.SS "Show installation status"
.EX
./depengine status
./depengine status --format=json
./depengine status --orphans
.EE
.SS "Remove a tool"
.EX
./depengine remove bat
./depengine remove --all --dry-run
.EE
.SS "Enable shell completion"
.EX
# Bash
source <(depengine completion bash)

# Zsh
source <(depengine completion zsh)

# Fish
depengine completion fish | source
.EE
